--TEST--
The two code points `rule:tooling/terminal-output-is-a-sink` passes through are the two that are not a column: a tab advances to the next stop wherever it stands, and a newline ends the row so the answer is the widest row rather than a sum — `rule:tooling/terminal-output-is-a-sink` and `rule:tooling/the-terminal-profile-resolves-once`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Both bounds are named together on purpose. A member that gave the tab a
// fixed width would print plausibly against either line below on its own: one
// column short of a stop and one column onto it are where a fixed count and a
// stop disagree, and a tab that lands exactly on a stop still advances a whole
// one rather than none.
echo "at seven=", Core\Cli::displayWidth("abcdefg\t|"), "\n";
echo "at eight=", Core\Cli::displayWidth("abcdefgh\t|"), "\n";

// The newline is the same shape of assertion from the other side: the rows are
// given in both orders, so an answer that took the first row, the last row or
// the sum of them fails one of the two while satisfying the other.
echo "narrow first=", Core\Cli::displayWidth("ab\n日本語"), "\n";
echo "wide first=", Core\Cli::displayWidth("日本語\nab"), "\n";

// The empty edge, which is a row of no columns and not an absence of rows.
echo "empty=", Core\Cli::displayWidth(""), "\n";
echo "newline alone=", Core\Cli::displayWidth("\n"), "\n";
--EXPECT--
at seven=9
at eight=17
narrow first=6
wide first=6
empty=0
newline alone=0
